# /etc/default/pacs-admin: settings for the helper service, the firewall, the disk assistant and the dashboard cron job.
# Deutsch: docs/install.de.md (Einstellungen), docs/reference.de.md (alle Variablen)
# Every commented line shows the built-in default; without this file all defaults apply. Copy the file with
#   cp /opt/pacs-admin/deploy/pacs-admin.env.example /etc/default/pacs-admin
# and uncomment only what you change. "bash /opt/pacs-admin/deploy/tools/preflight.sh" prints the values of your system
# (the table "my values" at its end).
#
# CHANGE-ME marks a setting that is wrong on many systems: check it, and delete the marker comment once you did.
#
# Rules for this file. The shell reads it (cron, pacs-storage.sh, manual runs) and so does systemd (the units); the two
# disagree on comments after a value, spaces and CRLF, so stay inside what both understand:
#   DO     one NAME=value per line, no spaces around =
#   DO     single quotes around a value with spaces, braces or JSON:  FW_PORT_NAMES='{"104": "DICOM (old)"}'
#   DO     plain whole numbers where a number is expected: STORAGE_MIN_GB=100 (not 100GB, 1.5 or 1e2). That is
#          PACS_SOCKET_UID, STORAGE_MIN_GB, STORAGE_FREE_GB and STORAGE_WARN_GB. The three STORAGE_* ones take at most 6
#          digits, PACS_SOCKET_UID at most 10. Anything else stops the program at once with a line like "STORAGE_FREE_GB=50GB ist keine ganze Zahl"
#          (not a whole number): in the journal for pacs-verwaltung, in /var/log/pacs-dashboard.log for the cron job,
#          on the screen for pacs-storage.sh
#   DO     LF line endings (a file saved on Windows has CRLF: sed -i 's/\r$//' /etc/default/pacs-admin)
#   DON'T  write "export", spaces around =, or a comment behind a value (FW_INTERFACE=eth0  # outside)
#   DON'T  use $VARIABLES or `commands` in values: only the shell would expand them
# A line with an empty value (FW_INTERFACE=) is the same as no line: the default applies.
# Check after every edit:  sh -n /etc/default/pacs-admin && echo syntax ok
# Apply:  systemctl restart pacs-verwaltung   (the cron job reads the file at its next run, every 5 minutes)
#         Not while a disk setup is running (the page shows it): the restart ends pacs-storage.sh half-way.
#         FW_* values need one more step:  set -a; . /etc/default/pacs-admin; set +a; python3 /opt/pacs-admin/server/firewall.py start
#         Never "systemctl restart pacs-firewall": it removes the filter first, so all ports are open for a moment.

# ---- General ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHANGE-ME if your dcm4chee compose project lives elsewhere: the directory with its docker-compose.yml; firewall.json and
# firewall/ are kept there too. Find it:
#   docker inspect -f '{{index .Config.Labels "com.docker.compose.project.working_dir"}}' arc
#PACS_DIR=/opt/dcm4chee-arc
# Working directory; status/status.json for the overview ("Uebersicht"; nginx serves it at /admin/daten/, mounted into the
# nginx container)
#PACS_ADMIN_VAR=/var/lib/pacs-admin
# Socket of the helper service (HTTP). nginx expects it inside its container as /run/pacs-admin/verwaltung.sock
#PACS_SOCKET=/run/pacs-admin/verwaltung.sock
# Socket for the log lines from nginx (syslog). Inside the container /run/pacs-admin/protokoll.sock, same directory
#PACS_LOG_SOCKET=/run/pacs-admin/protokoll.sock
# Owner of both sockets: the uid of nginx inside the nginx/OHIF container (101 in the official nginx and OHIF images). Ideally
# no account on the host has this uid ("getent passwd 101" prints nothing), otherwise every process of that account can use
# the service; the service warns at its start if one has. nginx installed on the host: the uid of its worker user (Debian:
# www-data = 33, deploy/nginx/README.md).
# A whole number; after a change: systemctl restart pacs-verwaltung
#PACS_SOCKET_UID=101
# Encrypted audit-log database (SQLCipher)
#PROTOKOLL_DB=/var/lib/pacs-protokoll/protokoll.db
# Key of the audit-log database, readable by root only; without the key the log cannot be read
#PROTOKOLL_KEY=/etc/pacs-protokoll.key

# ---- dcm4chee --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REST interface of dcm4chee as seen from the host
#ARC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
# AE title of the archive; must match aet in config.js and the AE title in the nginx files and app-config.js.
# Renaming it: run "bash /opt/pacs-admin/deploy/tools/set-aet.sh NEW-AET" (nginx files, app-config.js, config.js), then change
# this line
#ARC_AET=DCM4CHEE
# Device name of dcm4chee in LDAP (dicomDeviceName)
#ARC_DEVICE=dcm4chee-arc
# CHANGE-ME if your wildfly log is somewhere else: the log directory of wildfly ON THE HOST. server.log in it provides the
# DICOM and HL7 senders for the Firewall tab. It is the host side of the mount of /opt/wildfly/standalone in the arc
# container, plus /log. Find it:
#   docker inspect -f '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}' arc
# A wrong value makes the journal show an "absender_sammeln" error every few minutes and leaves the sender lists empty
# (journalctl -u pacs-verwaltung -n 30 --no-pager).
#ARC_LOG_DIR=/var/local/dcm4chee-arc/wildfly/log/
# CHANGE-ME if your containers are named differently (docker ps): name of the arc container (docker exec, check that arc
# sees a new disk)
#ARC_CONTAINER=arc
# CHANGE-ME if your containers are named differently: name of the LDAP container; ldapsearch/ldapmodify run inside it with
# its LDAP_ROOTDN/LDAP_ROOTPASS (or LDAP_ROOTPASS_FILE)
#LDAP_CONTAINER=ldap
# Base DN of the dcm4chee configuration in LDAP
#LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=dcm4che,dc=org

# ---- Storage (pacs-storage.sh, the "new disk" assistant) -------------------------------------------------------------
# Directory with the mount points; arc needs it as a volume with :rslave (new mounts without a restart)
#STORAGE_BASE=/mnt/fs
# Name of new disks: prefix plus two-digit number (PACS-Data01, PACS-Data02, ...), used as file system label and mount
# point. One letter, then letters, digits, - and _, at most 14 characters (an ext4 label has 16 with the number), otherwise
# pacs-storage.sh stops. The helper service only accepts disk names with this prefix (release, attach, detach).
# dashboard-status.py writes it as "prefix" into status.json; the overview shows the names without the prefix
#STORAGE_PREFIX=PACS-Data
# Owner of the new file system, uid:gid of wildfly in the dcm4chee image
#STORAGE_OWNER=1023:1023
# Smallest disk in GiB that the assistant offers to set up
#STORAGE_MIN_GB=100
# At least this many GB free to remove the "full" mark or to attach a disk to the write list. Also valid for the page:
# dashboard-status.py writes the value as "frei_gb" into status.json, and the overview shows "Markierung entfernen" (remove
# mark) and "In Schreibliste aufnehmen" (add to write list) only from this value on
#STORAGE_FREE_GB=50
# If the disks of the write list have less than this many GB free up to the threshold, the overview asks for a new disk
# (dashboard-status.py writes the value as "warn_gb" into status.json)
#STORAGE_WARN_GB=300
# Log of the assistant
#STORAGE_LOG=/var/log/pacs-storage.log

# ---- Firewall (firewall.py) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHANGE-ME unless the default route is the outside interface (find it: ip route show default, or preflight.sh): network
# interface towards the clients; empty (or no line) = interface of the default route (at boot, firewall.py start waits up to
# 60 s for it). Setting it explicitly is safer when the default route comes late (DHCP, allow-hotplug).
#FW_INTERFACE=
# Service ports as the clients reach them on the host (host port, for "104:11112" that is 104), separated by commas, the
# first one is the main port. Web: the first one is https; your own PC must keep access to it when you save.
# DICOM and HL7: the first one gets the suggestions under "Neu erkannt" (newly detected); firewall.py finds the
# senders in the server.log of arc through the ports inside the container (translated from docker ps). The three lists
# must not share a port, otherwise the defaults apply to all three
#FW_WEB_PORTS=443,80
#FW_DICOM_PORTS=11112,2762
#FW_HL7_PORTS=2575,12575
# JSON file (format like firewall.json) with the rule set to use as long as there is no firewall.json; empty = built in:
# FW_WEB_PORTS open, first DICOM and HL7 port in trial mode, the dcm4chee management ports (8080, 8443, 9990, 9993) and
# 5601, 8000, 9443 blocked, no allowed sources (remove from the built-in list what you do not run)
#FW_DEFAULT_RULES=
# your own names for ports, laid over the built-in ones, e.g. '{"104": "DICOM (old)", "8081": "Web service"}'
#FW_PORT_NAMES=
# Name patterns of the client PCs for the DNS search, separated by commas. {a..b} counts like in bash from a to b; if a
# bound has a leading zero, all numbers are padded with zeros to the width of the bounds. Example:
# 'pc{01..20},ws{001..040}' = pc01 to pc20 and ws001 to ws040; empty = no search
#FW_CLIENT_NAMES=

# ---- Overview (dashboard-status.py) ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Containers for the "Dienste" (services) tile, separated by commas or spaces; empty = all containers of the compose project in
# PACS_DIR (stopped ones in red)
#DASHBOARD_CONTAINERS=
